only when no removal succeeds at current granularity do we double granularity. But the phrase \"with slightly coarser granularity\" might be misinterpretation. Let's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nMaybe it means: after each successful removal, increase granularity by 1 (slightly coarser). Then when no removal succeeds at current granularity, double it. But \"slightly coarser\" vs \"double\" suggests two different modes. Alternatively, it could be a typo: \"Restart after an accepted removal\" might mean start over from the beginning of the list with the same granularity (since list changed). \"Slightly coarser\" might be miswritten. Or it could mean: after a removal, increase granularity by 1 (coarser but not double). Then after a pass with no removal, double granularity.\n\nGiven typical chunk reduction algorithms, often you try to remove chunks at a given granularity; if any chunk is removable, you remove it and then continue from the same granularity (maybe starting over from left). If no chunk is removable, you double the granularity. But here they say \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" – that indicates increasing granularity after each removal, not staying same. Could be a mix.\n\nLet's look at the expected output: \"reduced_items\" list of strings, \"attempts\" integer (number of removal attempts?), \"one_minimal\" boolean (whether the result is one-minimal, meaning no further single chunk can be removed while preserving required failure items? Possibly at the end we check if it's one-minimal at granularity 1? Or check that no chunk of any size can be removed? The name \"one_minimal\" suggests that at the smallest granularity (1) no removal is possible. Or that after the process, each item is required to keep required failure items.\n\nThe fixture provides some cases. Let's simulate with case 0: items = [\"t0\",\"t1\",\"t2\",\"t3\",\"t4\",\"t5\",\"t6\",\"t7\",\"t8\",\"t9\"], required_failure_items = [\"t0\",\"t6\"].\n\nStart granularity g=2. Chunks: [t0,t1], [t2,t3], [t4,t5], [t6,t7], [t8,t9]. Remove left to right. Check each chunk: remove [t0,t1] -> remaining items: t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Required items t0 and t6: t0 missing, so not acceptable. Next chunk [t2,t3] remove -> remaining: t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Contains t0 and t6? t0 present, t6 present => acceptable. So we accept removal of chunk [t2,t3]. Now list becomes [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Now restart with slightly coarser granularity? If we increase g by 1 (to 3), chunks: [t0,t1,t4], [t5,t6,t7], [t8,t9] (last partial). Try removal: remove first chunk [t0,t1,t4] -> remaining: t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Required t0 missing -> no. Next chunk [t5,t6,t7] -> remaining: t0,t1,t4,t8,t9. Contains t0 and t6? t6 is removed -> no. Next chunk [t8,t9] -> remaining: t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7. Contains both t0 and t6 -> yes. Accept removal of [t8,t9]. List becomes [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7]. Increase g to 4. Chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t5], [t6,t7]. Try removal first: remove [t0,t1,t4,t5] -> remaining: t6,t7. Missing t0 -> no. Next [t6,t7] -> remaining: t0,t1,t4,t5. Contains t0? yes, t6? no. So no removal at g=4. Double granularity to 8. Chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7] (since only 6 items). Remove that chunk -> empty list. Missing both required -> no. No removal. Double to 16, but list size 6, stop at singleton? \"stop at singleton granularity\" means when granularity becomes 1? Actually it says \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" Singleton granularity means g=1. So after g=8 fails, double to 16, but that's more than list length; we might stop when g > len(list), and then set g=1? Or stop at g=1 is the final step. The typical process: continue doubling until g exceeds list length, then try g=1. Or maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means the algorithm ends when we reach g=1 (i.e., try to remove individual items). Let's see: after g=8 fails, double to 16. Since 16 > 6, we go to singleton (g=1). At g=1, chunks are each item. Try left to right: remove t0 -> remaining: t1,t4,t5,t6,t7. Missing t0 -> no. remove t1 -> remaining: t0,t4,t5,t6,t7 -> contains both t0 and t6 -> yes. Accept removal of t1. List becomes [t0,t4,t5,t6,t7]. Restart with slightly coarser granularity: if we increase g by 1, g=2. G=2 chunks: [t0,t4], [t5,t6], [t7]. Try removal: [t0,t4] -> remaining t5,t6,t7 -> missing t0 -> no. [t5,t6] -> remaining t0,t4,t7 -> missing t6 -> no. [t7] -> remaining t0,t4,t5,t6 -> contains both -> yes. Accept removal of [t7]. List becomes [t0,t4,t5,t6]. Increase g to 3. Chunks: [t0,t4,t5], [t6]. Remove [t0,t4,t5] -> remaining t6 -> no. Remove [t6] -> remaining t0,t4,t5 -> contains t0, missing t6 -> no. No removal at g=3. Double to 6. Chunks: [t0,t4,t5,t6] (single chunk). Remove -> empty, missing both -> no. No removal. Double to 12 > 4, go to singleton g=1. At g=1, chunks: t0,t4,t5,t6. Try: remove t0 -> remaining t4,t5,t6 -> missing t0 -> no. remove t4 -> remaining t0,t5,t6 -> contains both? t0 yes, t6 yes -> accept. Remove t4. List becomes [t0,t5,t6]. Increase g to 2. Chunks: [t0,t5], [t6]. Remove [t0,t5] -> remaining [t6] -> no. Remove [t6] -> remaining [t0,t5] -> contains t0, missing t6 -> no. No removal at g=2. Double to 4 > len=3, go to singleton g=1. Chunks: t0, t5, t6. Try removal: remove t0 -> no. remove t5 -> remaining [t0,t6] -> contains both? yes. Accept removal of t5. List becomes [t0,t6]. Increase g to 2. Chunks: [t0,t6] (single chunk). Remove -> empty -> no (missing both). No removal at g=2. Double to 4 > 2, go to singleton g=1: chunks: t0, t6. Remove t0 -> remaining [t6] -> missing t0 -> no. Remove t6 -> remaining [t0] -> missing t6 -> no. No removal at g=1. So process ends. Final reduced_items = [\"t0\",\"t6\"]. one_minimal =