e if we ended because no removal at g=1? But the algorithm says stop at singleton granularity, meaning when we reach g=1 and attempt removals? The phrase: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" That could mean: continue doubling until granularity becomes 1 (i.e., when doubling makes it exceed list length? Or explicitly set g=1 after doubling fails). More likely: when no removal succeeds at current granularity, double it; if the doubled granularity would be > len(list) (or >1?), then set granularity to 1 (singleton) and attempt one more pass. If still no removal, stop. Then one_minimal is True.\n\nAlternatively, \"one_minimal\" could be a boolean indicating that after the process, the reduced list is minimal with respect to removal of any single chunk (i.e., at any granularity? That would be more complex). But given the name \"one_minimal\", it's likely about granularity 1.\n\nGiven the typical use in delta debugging or chunk reduction, \"1-minimal\" means no single element can be removed while preserving the property.\n\nThus, our algorithm:\n- Start with current_items = copy of case[\"items\"]\n- required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n- attempts = 0\n- granularity = 2\n- While True:\n    - If granularity > len(current_items): set granularity = 1  (singleton)\n    - Form chunks of size granularity (left to right). For each chunk in order:\n        - attempts += 1\n        - candidate = current_items with that chunk removed\n        - If all required items are in candidate (i.e., required subset of set(candidate)):\n            - Accept removal: current_items = candidate\n            - Break out of chunk loop (since we restart after accepted removal)\n    - If no removal was accepted in the loop:\n        - If granularity == 1: break (done)\n        - Else: granularity = granularity * 2 (double)\n    - Else (a removal was accepted):\n        - If granularity == 1: continue? But after removal at g=1, we should restart with slightly coarser granularity? The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That implies after any accepted removal, we increase granularity slightly (maybe by 1). But we haven't been doing that. The earlier manual simulation increased g by 1 after each acceptance. Let's re-evaluate.\n\nThe description: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: There are two types of granularity changes:\n- After a successful removal, we increase granularity by a small amount (\"slightly coarser\") - likely increment by 1.\n- After a full pass with no removal, we double granularity.\n- When granularity reaches 1 (singleton) after doubling? Or we stop when we cannot remove at g=1.\n\nOur manual simulation earlier used increment by 1 after success, doubling after failure. That gave a final result of [\"t0\",\"t6\"] which seems plausible as the minimal set containing both required items. Let's verify if that matches the \"one_minimal\" check: at the end, no single item removal works (since removing either t0 or t6 would lose the required item). So one_minimal True. Attempts count: many.\n\nWe need to implement that exactly.\n\nThus the algorithm:\n- items = list(case[\"items\"])\n- required_set = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n- attempts = 0\n- granularity = 2\n\n- While True:\n    - if granularity > len(items):\n        # If we are about to go to singleton after doubling? Actually according to the algorithm, double when no removal succeeds; after doubling, if granularity > len(items)? Then we set to singleton? The description: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So when we have no removal at current granularity, we double