ems\n    for r in required:\n        if r not in items:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required failure item {r} not in items\")\n    items = list(items)  # copy\n    required_set = set(required)\n    attempts = 0\n    granularity = 2\n    while True:\n        # if granularity > len(items), set to 1\n        if granularity > len(items):\n            granularity = 1\n        # create chunks (list of slices)\n        n = len(items)\n        removed = False\n        for start in range(0, n, granularity):\n            end = min(start + granularity, n)\n            attempts += 1\n            candidate = items[:start] + items[end:]\n            if required_set.issubset(candidate):\n                items = candidate\n                removed = True\n                break\n        if removed:\n            granularity += 1  # slightly coarser\n        else:\n            if granularity == 1:\n                break\n            granularity *= 2\n    # one_minimal check: try removing each single item\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i in range(len(items)):\n        candidate = items[:i] + items[i+1:]\n        if required_set.issubset(candidate):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": items\n    }\n\nBut note: The one_minimal check at the end might be redundant because the algorithm stops at g=1 with no removal. However, we should do it for safety if the algorithm might have stopped earlier? According to algorithm, it only stops when g=1 and no removal. So it's automatically True. But we can include the check anyway; it's not harmful.\n\nPotential issue: After the loop, items may still have changes? No.\n\nNow, we need to produce JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the Python source as a string.\n\nThe function must not use any imports beyond standard library, which is fine.\n\nNow, produce the final answer.\n\nWe need to adhere to the strict su